Message ordering in a distributed system is subtle. Network reordering, multi-datacenter replication, and concurrent messages can cause messages to arrive out of sequence at the gateway.

**Vector clocks** are the textbook answer for causal ordering in distributed systems, but they add complexity that isn't needed here. Each conversation is a single logical stream — not a multi-writer concurrent data structure. A simpler approach works: the server assigns a **monotonically increasing sequence number** per conversation using Redis `INCR` on `seq:{conversationId}`.

When the message service receives a message, it atomically increments the conversation's counter and stamps the message with that sequence number. Clients track the highest sequence number they've seen per conversation. If a client receives message 47 but their latest is 44, they know messages 45 and 46 are missing and fetch them from the API.

```java
@Service
public class MessageService {
    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private final CassandraTemplate cassandra;

    public Message send(UUID conversationId, UUID senderId, String content) {
        long seqNum = redis.opsForValue()
            .increment("seq:" + conversationId);

        Message msg = Message.builder()
            .conversationId(conversationId)
            .senderId(senderId)
            .content(content)
            .sequenceNumber(seqNum)
            .build();

        cassandra.insert(msg);
        return msg;
    }
}
```

This trades perfect causal ordering for operational simplicity. In the rare case where two messages are sent to the same conversation simultaneously from different senders, the server-assigned sequence resolves the order deterministically. Gap detection with a 5-second timeout triggers a fetch-from-API fallback, ensuring clients self-heal without manual intervention.

Group messaging introduces the **fan-out problem**: when User A sends a message to a 200-member group, how does it reach everyone?

**Fan-out on write** copies a message reference to each member's inbox. Reads are fast — each user reads from their own partition. The cost: a 200-member group means 200 writes per message, and large broadcast channels with 10,000+ subscribers make this prohibitively expensive.

**Fan-out on read** stores the message once in the group's timeline. Members query the group timeline when they open the chat. One write per message, but read contention grows with group popularity.

**The hybrid model** balances both. Small groups (≤ 100 members) use fan-out on write for instant delivery. Large channels and broadcast groups use fan-out on read, with only online members notified in real-time via their WebSocket connections.

```java
@Service
public class GroupDispatcher {
    private static final int FANOUT_THRESHOLD = 100;

    public void dispatch(Message msg, Group group) {
        cassandra.save(msg);

        if (group.getMemberCount() <= FANOUT_THRESHOLD) {
            group.getMembers().stream()
                .filter(id -> !id.equals(msg.getSenderId()))
                .forEach(id -> {
                    inboxWriter.append(id, msg.getMessageId());
                    pushToGateway(id, msg);
                });
        } else {
            notifyOnlineMembers(group, msg);
        }
    }
}
```

The 100-member threshold is tunable. WhatsApp limits groups to 1,024 and uses write-fan-out for all of them since even 1,024 writes per message is manageable. Discord, with channels exceeding 100,000 members, must use read-fan-out with efficient gateway-level push for online users.
